# What does NOT go into the build context.
#
# Beyond upload speed, two entries here are correctness rather than tidiness.
#
#   `.env.local` would be copied by `COPY . .` and Next loads it AFTER
#   `.env.production`, so it WINS. A developer's copy points at
#   http://localhost:8022; if it reached the build, the image would be built
#   against a host that does not exist in production, the build would still
#   succeed, and the site would fail at runtime with nothing to explain it.
#
#   `node_modules` from a Windows machine holds native binaries for the wrong
#   platform — sharp above all, which is what `next/image` needs — and `npm ci`
#   in the deps stage must be free to install the musl builds instead.

# Installed fresh inside the image, for linux/amd64 on alpine.
node_modules
**/node_modules

# Build output — produced by the build stage, never copied in.
.next
out
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ⚠ THE NATIVE TARGET IS THE BIGGEST WIN HERE. The Android and iOS projects carry
#   a Gradle cache, CocoaPods, and a copy of the last static export — hundreds of
#   megabytes that the web build never reads, and which would make every
#   `COPY . .` layer churn on each `cap sync`.
android
ios
.gradle

# ⚠ DEVELOPER AND APP-ONLY ENV FILES. `.env.production` is deliberately NOT
#   excluded: it is the configuration this image is built from. `.env.mobile`
#   belongs to `npm run build:native` and would only confuse this one.
.env.local
.env.*.local
.env.development
.env.mobile
.env.test

# Vercel's local state — this deploys to Dokploy, not Vercel.
.vercel

# History and tooling the build never reads.
.git
.github
.vscode
.idea
*.log
npm-debug.log*
coverage

# Docs and planning.
*.md
!README.md

# OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
